{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-85060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/0d91ac9127718b0f2755ec14c268d23af810c2ec6f13b4935930b4b348b76b34.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{9}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/ec2b27e99ad03df281464b1a1fb08b5e8cbf869acf1892a815d9031bf506dafe.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the third quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{-\\pi /2}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/97dfdde016e4c3482fe036379e22aa95af27c1bcf93076ea9054e263100ad8e1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/6f7f37b10d483f7ce2bf147f316195b4be578ae62da8538a3aac4a8dbfa2ec1c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83980" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/dc35d0362ef95d11402ad44cded01abf46686d3088e9d852bd0b9a7ba2454e16.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/acb2f50d01df6ebec6003cc7b9c944b759113b9f364455fb62eede389b335229.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},-3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/5991463a56d8358164d32b15f73e938573463d8d706d2b9c8fc06803326a5f0f.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/bf7089294bc72330065590a9fe54acebee6d0ab982bffb4a162407c7232898ec.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2-\\pi "] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/b0e8a196dc27f333db4179b5c318ccd3aa4b385c0752c570fbfedc0226b3b15b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{72}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the half-annulus between the disks of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/da18aafec278b5d44b0530097729e4cb4b319e219c005fc155d78fab8aa90937.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{81}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =136\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{272}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "136"] ["math" "272"] ["math" "\\frac{136}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{272}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-161121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{272}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/d0d72be437a462f5eea781e83cc2a95e956e11ddc3295ad3b02a4a57867d9271.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{3},-1).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/2386c1dcb1cf71231f2bb508dc1dd85dadef15b806eaf7f9a9ad0d3e4f3da1ca.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6} \\\\  & =[(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(0-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2e-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(e-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-83999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-12\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cdde8a3755704e2d1eb7ff384bce8b809dd3689163b6183da02fd2518037a22"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "12\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}+5"] ["math" "\\pi \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "q-85204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b4dc50b9fce6de92c95b1d8f73ab0be3a938a9c31fb6f58f0033f2768194d1"} {"question" "e-7411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7411.json" "file_sha256" "112047d23434c9be23f68452ac419a4ad08eabf8050ede078cdca278df7c99d5"} {"question" "e-7411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7411.json" "file_sha256" "112047d23434c9be23f68452ac419a4ad08eabf8050ede078cdca278df7c99d5"} {"question" "e-7409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/4e47cfcf3da66707e6bc898d099868bfbd6d5163dcd38d47e4c79b2180e89ec6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7409.json" "file_sha256" "81b1b547d5d780091830f07a3b63038c8f7a0be757b0844def17fe2c62b8b895"} {"question" "e-7409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7409.json" "file_sha256" "81b1b547d5d780091830f07a3b63038c8f7a0be757b0844def17fe2c62b8b895"} {"question" "e-7410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7410.json" "file_sha256" "a4c46229918d6b1c955f1f6ef11e23d043d89c5f891de1c86e421a4558576a48"} {"question" "e-7410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/example-7410.json" "file_sha256" "a4c46229918d6b1c955f1f6ef11e23d043d89c5f891de1c86e421a4558576a48"}] "usage" {"e-7410" [] "q-85059" [] "q-83980" [] "e-7411" [] "q-161102" [] "e-7409" []} "basis" 1837 "retractions" [[17592186157787 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-85059.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2$, as shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}(x - y)dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186157787 :question/worked-solution "The upper half-annulus has $1\\le r\\le2$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. The integrand becomes $x-y=r(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$, and the area element adds another factor $r$. Therefore $$\\iint_D(x-y)\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_1^2r^2(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186157787 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186157778 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-83980.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$ and the line $x = 2\\sqrt{2}$, as shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}\\frac{y}{x}dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186157778 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows the part in the first quadrant to the right of $x=2\\sqrt2$ and inside $r=4$. The line becomes $r=2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta$, meeting the circle at angle $\\pi/4$. Thus $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/4$ and $2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le4$. Since $y/x=\\tan\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, the integral is $$\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\tan\\theta\\int_{2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta}^4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186157547 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-161102.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\underset{D}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} {)}^{2}} \\, \\text{d} A ,$, where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3$, as shown above."] [17592186157547 :question/worked-solution "The full annulus has $2\\le r\\le3$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$. The integrand $1/(x^2+y^2)^2$ becomes $r^{-4}$, and the area element adds $r$. Thus $$\\iint_D\\frac{dA}{(x^2+y^2)^2}=2\\pi\\int_2^3r^{-3}\\,dr=2\\pi\\left[-\\frac1{2r^2}\\right]_2^3=2\\pi\\left(\\frac18-\\frac1{18}\\right)=\\frac{5\\pi}{36}.$$"] [17592186157547 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186157037 :question/problem "The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4$, as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/dab6cca85da8e22d877207792771a513.png)"] [17592186157037 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\underset{D}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}} \\, \\text{d} A & = \\underset{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{( r \\sin   \\theta {)}^{2} + ( r \\cos   \\theta {)}^{2}} \\, r \\, \\text{d} r \\text{d} \\theta \\\\  & = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi  / 2} {\\int }_{3}^{4} \\frac{r}{{r}^{2} ( {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta + {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta )} \\, \\text{d} r \\, \\text{d} \\theta \\\\  & = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi  / 2} {\\int }_{3}^{4} \\frac{1}{r} \\, \\text{d} r \\, \\text{d} \\theta  .\\end{array}$$"] [17592186157035 :question/problem "The region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/45342b77b0830e7179c7f7275385a285.png)\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A$"] [17592186157035 :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. The circle $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ becomes $r^2+2r\\cos\\theta=0$, with outer boundary $r=-2\\cos\\theta$. The line $x=-1$ becomes $r=-\\sec\\theta$. The diagram shows the part above the $x$-axis to the left of this line; its angles run from the intersection $(-1,1)$ at $3\\pi/4$ to $\\pi$. Thus $$\\frac{3\\pi}4\\le\\theta\\le\\pi,\\qquad-\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le-2\\cos\\theta.$$ Since $xy\\,dA=r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, $$\\iint_Dxy\\,dA=\\int_{3\\pi/4}^{\\pi}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\int_{-\\sec\\theta}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186157036 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$, where the region $D$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/a14cb4ba64dd6df3dde2fc1568d8d921.png)"] [17592186157036 :question/worked-solution "The circle $x^2+(y-1/2)^2=1/4$ becomes $r=\\sin\\theta$. The line $y=1/2$ becomes $r=\\tfrac12\\csc\\theta$. The diagram shows the first-quadrant part above the line, so $\\pi/4\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$ and $\\tfrac12\\csc\\theta\\le r\\le\\sin\\theta$. The transformed integrand including the area element is $(2r\\sin\\theta/r^2)r=2\\sin\\theta$. Therefore $$\\begin{aligned}I&=\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{\\frac12\\csc\\theta}^{\\sin\\theta}2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}(2\\sin^2\\theta-1)\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\left[-\\frac12\\sin(2\\theta)\\right]_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}=\\frac12.\\end{aligned}$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186153858 17592186153859 17592186153860 17592186153861 17592186153862 17592186155921 17592186155922 17592186155923 17592186155924 17592186155925 17592186155975 17592186155976 17592186155977 17592186155978 17592186155979] "immutable_fields" [[17592186153857 :answer-field/correct] [17592186153857 :answer-field/id] [17592186153857 :answer-field/key] [17592186153857 :answer-field/type] [17592186155920 :answer-field/correct] [17592186155920 :answer-field/id] [17592186155920 :answer-field/key] [17592186155920 :answer-field/type] [17592186155974 :answer-field/correct] [17592186155974 :answer-field/id] [17592186155974 :answer-field/key] [17592186155974 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
